# Line endings are a property of the repo, not of whichever machine wrote a file last.
# The index holds LF for every text file, and eol=lf makes every checkout hold LF too, on
# every platform. It overrides a machine core.autocrlf, so a working tree cannot drift to
# CRLF and no file can end up half-CRLF because one tool appended LF to a CRLF file. That
# matters most for anything a POSIX shell reads: bash takes a CRLF `export HOME=/root` as
# a path with a trailing CR and corrupts everything derived from it (native/gorun.sh).
* text=auto eol=lf

# Applied against LF sources, so the patch itself has to stay LF.
*.patch -text

*.wasm binary
